>>> DEFECT D1 (NOT a criterion failure — reported for the lead) <<<
The NON-MUTATION half of the shipped TestWarningsKey_OrderInvariant is
VACUOUS. It reads:
    a := []string{"a", "b"}
    ...
    if a[0] != "a" || a[1] != "b" { t.Errorf("warningsKey mutated its input...") }
`a` is ALREADY in sorted order, so an in-place sort leaves it identical and the
assertion cannot fire. Proven by fault injection (PROBE D, scratch copy:
`sorted := make(...); copy(...)` replaced by `sorted := warnings`, i.e. sorting
the caller's slice IN PLACE):
$ go test -count=1 -v -run 'TestWarningsKey_OrderInvariant' ./internal/handlers/accounts/
  --- PASS: TestWarningsKey_OrderInvariant (0.00s)     <-- stays GREEN
$ go test -count=1 -v -run 'TestCHK3B_' ./internal/handlers/accounts/   (my mirror)
  zz_chk3b_test.go:13: CHK3B before=[zzz-last aaa-first mmm-mid]
                             after=[aaa-first mmm-mid zzz-last]
  --- FAIL: TestCHK3B_NonMutation_UnsortedInput
  --- PASS: TestCHK3B_ShippedAssertionShape_SortedInput  <-- the shipped shape,
                                                             green under the bug
So the IMPLEMENTATION is non-mutating (proved in (b) and (c) above, which is
what criterion 7 asserts, hence PASS), but the shipped regression guard would
not catch a future edit that drops the copy. One-word fix: assert on `b`
(which is ["b","a"]) instead of / as well as `a`.

