==================================================================
CRITERION 9 — the worker's excluded paths (anchor add/delete).     PASS
==================================================================
Verified against the handlers, not accepted on the worker's word.
(a) STATIC. I read handleAddAnchor and handleDeleteAnchor in full. Inside
    accounts.Mutate, the ONLY fields either one writes are
      accts[i].Anchors = filtered
      accts[i].UpdatedAt = time.Now()
    Neither adds nor removes an element of accts, and neither touches
    FilePatterns. (`sed -n '/func handleDeleteAnchor/,/^}/p' ... | grep 'accts\[i\]\.'`
    yields exactly those two lines; same for handleAddAnchor.)
(b) STATIC, other side of the dependency. OverlapWarnings(accts, basenames)
    (internal/services/accounts/accounts.go) iterates basenames x sortedByID
    (accts) and calls accountMatches(a, name), which reads ONLY a.FilePatterns.
    Nothing in either function reads Anchors or UpdatedAt. So the warning set
    is a pure function of (account existence, FilePatterns, CSV basenames) —
    none of which an anchor mutation can change. The claim holds.
(c) DYNAMIC, and the important half: excluded from *resolving* does NOT mean
    excluded from *reporting*. Both anchor handlers call buildPageData(r)
    followed by renderList(w, r, data) on the success path, so they must serve
    the CURRENT warning set. Proved by resolving the overlap out-of-band and
    then running an anchor delete:
$ go test -count=1 -v -run 'TestCHK3_AnchorPathsCannotResolveButDoReportCurrentState' ...
  zz_chk3_test.go:242: CHK3 anchor-add warnings=[file "usaa-checking.csv"
    matches multiple accounts (a-wide, b-narrow); "a-wide" wins by ID order]
  zz_chk3_test.go:260: CHK3 anchor-delete after out-of-band resolve:
    Warnings=[] Key=""
  --- PASS
An anchor mutation neither invents nor caches a warning: it reports exactly
what the store says right now. Not writing a "resolving" test for the anchor
paths is therefore correct — such a test could not be written, because no
anchor mutation can move the warning set.

==================================================================
CRITERION 10 — adjudicating the disclosed workaround.
      JUDGEMENT: the test is asserting something MEANINGFUL, but its
      MECHANISM is a brittle whole-body string scan and it WILL misfire
      again. Reported as DEFECT D2; not a criterion failure.
==================================================================
FACTS FIRST. The literal announcement string in the template is now
  web/templates/pages/accounts.html:184:  region.textContent = 'Account
      overlap warning dismissed.';
and that <script> lives in the accounts-content block, i.e. it is served on
EVERY /accounts response regardless of warning state. Demonstrated:
$ go test -count=1 -v -run 'TestCHK3_NoOverlapPage_ScriptCopyIsInScannedBody' ...
  CHK3 no-overlap body contains 'Pattern overlap warning' = false
  CHK3 no-overlap body contains dismissal announcement literal = true
  CHK3 no-overlap body contains accounts-warnings-region = true
  CHK3 with NATURAL wording, no-overlap body would contain 'Pattern overlap
    warning' = true
(the last line substitutes "Pattern overlap warning dismissed." back into the
served bytes and re-scans — it trips.) So the worker's disclosure is accurate:
the natural wording really would have turned
TestHandlePage_RendererMode_NoOverlapRendersNoWarningBlock red.

IS THE TEST STILL MEANINGFUL? Yes. It is not a dead assertion. Its scan is
gated on three genuinely warning-conditional things, all inside {{if .Warnings}}:
the visible banner heading ">Pattern overlap warning<", the sr-only region text
"Pattern overlap warning: ...", and the data-warnings-text attribute. PROBE E
(force warnings non-empty) turned it FAIL, so it is non-vacuous and it does
guard the real "no overlap => no warning UI anywhere" property.

BUT THE MECHANISM IS A DEFECT (D2). The assertion is
`strings.Contains(wholeBody, "Pattern overlap warning")` over the ENTIRE
response, which includes an always-rendered <script> block whose JS `//`
comments and string literals are served verbatim. That makes unrelated script
copy a hostage of a test about warning rendering. Consequences already visible:
  - it forced a copy change that has nothing to do with the property under
    test, and
  - the substitute wording is now internally inconsistent with the rest of the
    UI: the banner heading says "Pattern overlap warning", the dismiss control
    says aria-label="Dismiss pattern overlap warning", and the confirmation
    the user then hears says "Account overlap warning dismissed." — a phrase
    that appears nowhere else on the page. (Whether that mismatch is an a11y
    problem in its own right is checker-a11y's call, not mine; I note it only
    because this test caused it.)
  - the next person who writes any script comment or literal containing the
    phrase gets a red test with a misleading failure message.
A test should not be able to fail for a reason unrelated to what it is named
for. The property is better pinned by scoped assertions the same file already
uses elsewhere: assert `strings.Count(body, ">Pattern overlap warning<") == 0`,
`!strings.Contains(body, "id=\"accounts-warnings-banner\"")`, and
`strings.Contains(body, "data-warnings-text=\"\"")` — same coverage, no
constraint on script copy, and a failure message that says which thing broke.
RECOMMENDATION: accept attempt 3 as-is (the workaround is disclosed, harmless
to the property, and the tree is green), and fix D2 as a follow-up rather than
sending S4 back for it. Fixing D2 would also let the announcement text be
restored to "Pattern overlap warning dismissed.", matching the control's own
accessible name.

